From 8e55d24071eaf03c6e5d2f25695b6abbb66d8c81 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 17:41:29 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(kanban): never replay a spent park's wake when a remote
 re-park is imported row-first [daveey]

Board t_00d7ca7eed7bf381: 5 of 516 parks in 24 h (2026-11-14/15) were woken 0-5 s after a worker parked them,
by the card's home gateway, carrying the PREVIOUS park's wake_at. Clearest case, hub t_e09068fc5f85f215 at
12:59:21: event 1252839 `continuation_woken wake_at=1794742440` (a replay of the 11:34:20 wake of park 1250787,
run 179035) landed one id before worker-5's `continued` 1252840 (run 179096, wake_at 1794748461 + a CI
wake_check); the card sat ready through its CI wait. Run 179096's `claimed`/`spawned` (1252798-1252802) and the
11:34:20 wake (1251745) were already on the hub.

Five whys
1. Why woken at once? `_wake_parks` saw `status='scheduled'` and `pending_wake` returned park 1250787, whose
   wake_at was hours past.
2. Why that park? `park_state` takes the newest `continued`/`scheduled` event; the new park's event was not
   on the hub yet.
3. Why was the row there without its event? `kanban_db_sync.apply_remote_changes` applies one feed page per
   transaction; the task row and the `continued` event can fall on different pages, and the dispatcher tick ran
   between them (row first; event first is harmless: the row is still `running`).
4. Why did nothing notice the park was spent? `park_state` never checked that a wake or a later run's claim
   had already consumed the event it returned. `restore_missing_checks` already had that fence for probes
   ("a later run's park has not reached this board yet"), the park sweep did not.
5. Why no in-transaction guard? `_wake_parks` read the park outside the write txn and only rechecked status.

Fix (hermes_cli/kanban_continuation.py)
- `park_state` treats a parking event as spent when a `continuation_woken`, or a `claimed`/`spawned` of a
  different run, follows it (`_park_spent`). A spent park is reported `superseded`, never `timed`: the sweep
  waits for the card's own park instead of replaying the old wake.
- `missing_wake` gives a superseded card a late backstop (`SUPERSEDED_PARK_GRACE_SECONDS`, 1 h after its last
  event) so a lost park event cannot strand it; `blocks_for_human` never blocks such a card on a block kind
  left from before its run. The backstop wake is marked `park_missing`.
- `_wake_parks` and `_enforce_wake_invariant` re-read the park inside the write transaction and skip if it
  moved or was spent since the read.

Tests: tests/hermes_cli/test_kanban_remote_repark.py (new). RED on origin/main: 5 failed / 2 passed (the
event-first controls pass). GREEN: 7 passed. Consumers (every test file importing kanban_continuation,
kanban_wake_check, kanban_wake_summary, kanban_timed_park, wake_due_continuations): 110 files, 2719 passed,
0 failed.
